WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:04.036
-Our first guest tonight is an
Oscar- and Tony-nominated

00:00:04.036 --> 00:00:06.473
and Emmy-winning actress you
know from shows such as

00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:07.774
"John Adams" and "The Big C"

00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:10.509
and films like "The Truman Show"
and "Love Actually."

00:00:10.509 --> 00:00:12.778
She stars in
the final season of "Ozark,"

00:00:12.778 --> 00:00:14.514
which is available now
on Netflix.

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:15.548
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:17.683
-Let me be exceedingly clear.

00:00:17.683 --> 00:00:20.387
I don't want to
work for you anymore.

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:23.256
-Mm...

00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:25.258
I don't think you would have
grabbed on to my coattails

00:00:25.258 --> 00:00:27.660
if you didn't think
I was going somewhere.

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:31.731
You've worked
your whole life for this.

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:33.133
-I...

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:38.504
I could have been killed.

00:00:38.504 --> 00:00:40.106
-But you weren't.

00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:43.710
-Please welcome to the show,
Laura Linney, everybody.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.179
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:04.563
Welcome to the show, Laura!

00:01:04.563 --> 00:01:06.398
-Yay!
-I'm so glad you're here.

00:01:06.398 --> 00:01:08.168
-I'm so happy to be here.

00:01:08.168 --> 00:01:11.637
-This is -- It must be
a little sad for you

00:01:11.637 --> 00:01:13.072
to say goodbye to
the character of Wendy.

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:15.842
-Yeah, it is.
-I mean, what a juicy role.

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:18.177
-Oh, she was so fun.
-Yeah.

00:01:18.177 --> 00:01:20.045
-Oh, she was just out there
and badly behaved.

00:01:20.045 --> 00:01:21.447
-Yeah.
-It was just delicious.

00:01:21.447 --> 00:01:24.584
-So it is true, what we always
hear actors say, which is,

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:26.452
it's really fun
to play terrible people.

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:28.021
-It is. It is.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:30.857
-It gives you permission to
just slide all over the place,

00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:32.659
and just, like,
ethically and emotionally,

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:34.460
just be just sort of chaos.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.697
-And, so -- And I'm assuming
you never brought

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:38.698
any of Wendy home to
your regular life.

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:39.732
-Never.

00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:41.433
-Yeah, very easy to
leave her on set?

00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:44.904
-My husband's so happy
with me right now.

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:46.472
-Well, right,
because now he has, like --

00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:47.706
There's actual visual proof
of how bad you can be

00:01:47.706 --> 00:01:48.908
if given the opportunity.

00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:52.244
-And now I'm missing her,
so every once in a while,

00:01:52.244 --> 00:01:55.014
maybe I just, like,
slip it on a little bit,

00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:57.983
just, like, really -- And it's
our anniversary tonight,

00:01:57.983 --> 00:01:59.085
so maybe just, you know...

00:01:59.085 --> 00:02:00.119
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:02.388
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:04.156
-Happy anniversary.

00:02:04.156 --> 00:02:06.125
So, I would imagine
it was also --

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:08.061
You shot the show in Georgia.

00:02:08.061 --> 00:02:09.229
-In Atlanta, yeah.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:11.997
-And they must be
a little sad, as well, like,

00:02:11.997 --> 00:02:13.466
the fact that you guys
went down there and created

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:14.800
this great show that --

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.303
-I hope so, but there's
so much going on in Atlanta.

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:19.205
I mean, it's a
really exciting place to be.

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:20.973
My whole family's
from the state of Georgia,

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:23.143
so although I was born up here,

00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:25.344
I have a lot of
history in Georgia.

00:02:25.344 --> 00:02:28.214
So I loved being there,
and it's so exciting to see

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:30.883
that city explode
in the way that it is.

00:02:30.883 --> 00:02:32.184
-Yeah, that's really cool.
-Yeah.

00:02:32.184 --> 00:02:34.186
-You mentioned you have
family from Georgia.

00:02:34.186 --> 00:02:35.821
You have an 8-year-old son.
-I do.

00:02:35.821 --> 00:02:37.357
-Are you trying to impress

00:02:37.357 --> 00:02:39.758
Georgia sports,
Georgia football on him?

00:02:39.758 --> 00:02:42.895
-Well, we have a connection to
the University of Georgia,

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:44.163
so, Bulldogs.

00:02:44.163 --> 00:02:46.732
-Congratulations.
-Thank you very much. Thank you.

00:02:46.732 --> 00:02:48.133
And my great friend
Willow Sparks,

00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:52.304
who's basically family,
is indoctrinating him fiercely.

00:02:52.304 --> 00:02:53.706
-That's a good age.

00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:55.841
I think 8 is a good time to
start drilling it in, yeah.

00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:57.010
-Oh, yeah, he's right in.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.945
-So, I didn't realize
you have a connection to

00:02:58.945 --> 00:03:00.413
my home state of New Hampshire

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:01.848
and the New Hampshire
theater scene.

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:03.082
-I do. I do.

00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:04.850
-So, what made you
first start going up there?

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:07.787
-Well, my father, in 1953,
was an apprentice at

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:09.455
the New London Barn Playhouse
in New London, New Hampshire.

00:03:09.455 --> 00:03:11.090
[ Crowd chants cheers ]
Yes, hooray.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:15.361
And then, when I was a teenager,
I spent my --

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:16.996
My parents split
when I was an infant.

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:18.764
I would spend the summers
with my father.

00:03:18.764 --> 00:03:20.266
And, you know,
you don't really want to

00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:21.567
hang out with
your parents all the time.

00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:24.003
And so I begged him
to let me work at the theater.

00:03:24.003 --> 00:03:25.271
I begged him.

00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:27.606
So he took me there,
and they said to, you know,

00:03:27.606 --> 00:03:29.041
the guy who ran
the theater like,

00:03:29.041 --> 00:03:30.109
"Just let her work
for a few hours.

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:31.277
She'll come home."

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:34.313
And I was there 15 hours a day,
every day.

00:03:34.313 --> 00:03:37.684
I broke every
child-labor law known to man.

00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:39.685
I loved it.

00:03:39.685 --> 00:03:42.855
And I was 11, 12, and 13
when I worked there.

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:43.890
And then I also worked at

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.991
the Keene Summer Theatre
in Keene.

00:03:45.991 --> 00:03:47.459
So I love New Hampshire.

00:03:47.459 --> 00:03:51.397
-Now, tell me, when you were 11,
did you already love theater

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:52.932
or did you fall in love with it
by doing that?

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:54.200
-I already loved it.
-You already loved it, yeah.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.703
-It was like the mother ship.
It just called me.

00:03:56.703 --> 00:03:58.137
-Yeah. That's so nice to --

00:03:58.137 --> 00:04:00.139
-I was running lights at 11.

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:02.308
It was ridiculous.
It was insane.

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.377
-It's a very libertarian state,
so I'm sure

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:06.745
when it comes to labor laws,
they're like,

00:04:06.745 --> 00:04:07.779
"Who's going to come?

00:04:07.779 --> 00:04:08.781
Who's going to check?"

00:04:08.781 --> 00:04:10.750
-I was so happy.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.585
I was around all these
older actors,

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:15.788
who must have been
freshmen in college, probably.

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:17.556
So they were like the, you know,
Mount Olympus.

00:04:17.556 --> 00:04:18.991
Like, these gods would walk --

00:04:18.991 --> 00:04:21.327
They did "Anything Goes"
and they did "Oliver,"

00:04:21.327 --> 00:04:22.528
and, oh, God, I was in heaven.

00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:23.796
-That's wonderful.
-Yeah, it was great.

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:26.932
-So, you know, a show like this,
a show like "Ozark,"

00:04:26.932 --> 00:04:28.634
which I feel like, you know,
people loved

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:30.335
before the pandemic,
and then it was a show

00:04:30.335 --> 00:04:31.738
that I feel like anybody
who hadn't started it found it.

00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:33.005
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:33.005 --> 00:04:35.207
-And so everybody has
this relationship with it,

00:04:35.207 --> 00:04:36.475
and, obviously, it's sort of --

00:04:36.475 --> 00:04:39.211
I think to describe it as
a thrill ride is fair.

00:04:39.211 --> 00:04:40.646
You're also a producer on it.

00:04:40.646 --> 00:04:43.616
How much thought did
you guys put into the ending

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:45.384
and how important it would be
to a show like this?

00:04:45.384 --> 00:04:47.120
-Well, the ending --
I let the writers

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.523
and our magnificent showrunner,
Chris Mundy, like, handle that.

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:54.827
You know, when someone has
created a full four-season arc,

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:56.395
like, the architect
of something like that,

00:04:56.395 --> 00:04:59.031
you want them to be able to tell
the story they want to tell.

00:04:59.031 --> 00:05:00.966
So I just said,
"Yes, thank you."

00:05:00.966 --> 00:05:03.736
And, you know, they were just
amazing for four seasons.

00:05:03.736 --> 00:05:07.073
Like, every season had something
special for all of us to do.

00:05:07.073 --> 00:05:08.607
It was an amazing ensemble,

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:10.676
and, you know,
we loved doing it

00:05:10.676 --> 00:05:12.678
and we're so happy
that people seem to like it.

00:05:12.678 --> 00:05:15.214
And, you know, it was
really a real privilege.

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:16.882
-Do you think about --
When you think about

00:05:16.882 --> 00:05:18.717
what you're going to do next,
do you try to go as

00:05:18.717 --> 00:05:20.219
far afield from a character
like Wendy as possible?

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:21.620
-No, it's going to be hard.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:24.857
I'm actually -- I'm leaving soon
to go do a movie in Ireland,

00:05:24.857 --> 00:05:26.825
and I've actually
thought about, like,

00:05:26.825 --> 00:05:31.096
"I've got to get certain
cadences out of my mind.

00:05:31.096 --> 00:05:34.467
I have to --" You know, it'll be
interesting to see if I'm --

00:05:34.467 --> 00:05:36.969
Because I've never played
anyone for that long,

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:39.805
so I don't know
how haunted I will be.

00:05:39.805 --> 00:05:42.041
-Yeah. Well, that's going to be
a fun thing to find out.

00:05:42.041 --> 00:05:43.909
-I'm sure people
will let me know.

00:05:43.909 --> 00:05:45.678
-You're going to close,
like, you know,

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:46.979
the medicine cabinet
in the bathroom

00:05:46.979 --> 00:05:48.113
and just be looking back at
yourself, being like,

00:05:48.113 --> 00:05:49.115
"Wendy's still here."

00:05:49.115 --> 00:05:50.116
-I know.
-Yeah.

00:05:50.116 --> 00:05:52.751
-I'm gonna rip something
off a wall.

00:05:52.751 --> 00:05:54.887
-Hey, I'm want to pitch
a show for everybody

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:57.490
who's done with "Ozark"
and is like --

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:00.058
I love the miniseries
"John Adams" so much.

00:06:00.058 --> 00:06:01.160
-Oh, thank you.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:02.428
-And, see,
that was a very nice person.

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:03.462
You got to play
a nice person in that one.

00:06:03.462 --> 00:06:05.364
-Yes, I did. I sure did. Yeah.

00:06:05.364 --> 00:06:07.366
-And I feel like
there was just something --

00:06:07.366 --> 00:06:09.068
I rewatched it again
during the pandemic,

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:10.803
and because it is
people who, like,

00:06:10.803 --> 00:06:12.171
believe in
what's good about America,

00:06:12.171 --> 00:06:13.472
it's a really nice thing
to watch again.

00:06:13.472 --> 00:06:14.606
-Isn't it?
-Yeah.

00:06:14.606 --> 00:06:16.442
-It was a
wonderful series to do,

00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:18.678
and I learned so much
about our country.

00:06:18.678 --> 00:06:20.012
You know,
it was just shameful that,

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:21.414
you know, at least
when I was in high school,

00:06:21.414 --> 00:06:24.550
like, American history
was pretty light.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:26.085
You didn't really learn stuff.

00:06:26.085 --> 00:06:30.189
And you should sort of learn it
again as a more mature adult...

00:06:30.189 --> 00:06:31.290
-Yeah, when you
have perspective on it.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.459
-...to learn how
our country came together,

00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:36.228
how these people, these flawed,
wonderful human beings,

00:06:36.228 --> 00:06:38.497
created this system that tried
and some things failed

00:06:38.497 --> 00:06:40.266
and some things succeeded.

00:06:40.266 --> 00:06:43.969
And then just
the human commonality

00:06:43.969 --> 00:06:48.040
that we still have with
the people then and there.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:51.377
-It is also -- It's a really
wonderful show about a marriage.

00:06:51.377 --> 00:06:54.614
Like, I think the selling point
is about American history,

00:06:54.614 --> 00:06:56.615
and then you watch it again --
I also have the perspective

00:06:56.615 --> 00:06:57.683
of being married
the second time,

00:06:57.683 --> 00:06:58.817
and I was like, "Oh, this part's

00:06:58.817 --> 00:07:01.320
way more interesting
than America, yeah."

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.422
Or certainly more complicated.
-Sure.

00:07:03.422 --> 00:07:04.556
Yeah, well, it makes --
And you realize

00:07:04.556 --> 00:07:06.892
there are certain things
that just span time.

00:07:06.892 --> 00:07:09.594
Like, they -- I always loved
the moment where she went --

00:07:09.594 --> 00:07:11.997
where they went to Europe

00:07:11.997 --> 00:07:15.000
and they came back
and she was famous.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.836
And, all of a sudden,
you realize, like,

00:07:16.836 --> 00:07:21.006
her behavior really shifted
because she became famous.

00:07:21.006 --> 00:07:23.475
She could feel
the power of fame.

00:07:23.475 --> 00:07:25.544
This very together,

00:07:25.544 --> 00:07:27.747
sort of modest woman,
all of a sudden,

00:07:27.747 --> 00:07:29.015
had a little blood
in her mouth for power.

00:07:29.015 --> 00:07:30.315
And I thought that was like,

00:07:30.315 --> 00:07:32.484
"Oh, I've seen other people
go through that, as well.

00:07:32.484 --> 00:07:34.386
That's not an uncommon story."

00:07:34.386 --> 00:07:35.487
-So it turns out it's not just

00:07:35.487 --> 00:07:36.789
Hollywood where that happens.

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:40.292
-It's not. Nope, nope.
-So, again, 8-year-old son.

00:07:40.292 --> 00:07:42.260
I would imagine
"Ozark" is not on the table yet.

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:44.329
-No, no.
None of it's on the table.

00:07:44.329 --> 00:07:46.131
-Nothing.
Yeah, I was trying to think.

00:07:46.131 --> 00:07:48.667
Do you have anything --
No real entry point, yeah.

00:07:48.667 --> 00:07:49.902
-No, not yet. Teenage --

00:07:49.902 --> 00:07:51.870
-Does he even believe
you're an actor?

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:53.372
-He doesn't quite get it.
-Yeah.

00:07:53.372 --> 00:07:56.942
-He knows that, like, I have
a lot of friends on the street.

00:07:56.942 --> 00:07:59.011
-Yeah.
-Like, people want to say "hi."

00:07:59.011 --> 00:08:00.412
They're very friendly,
and people are nice.

00:08:00.412 --> 00:08:01.814
They come up and they say "hi."

00:08:01.814 --> 00:08:04.449
And every once in a while, he'll
be like, "How do they know you?"

00:08:04.449 --> 00:08:06.819
I'm like, "Oh, you know,
I show up at work."

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:08.488
But, you know...
-That's a cool thing.

00:08:08.488 --> 00:08:09.621
It's a cool thing
to be able to explain.

00:08:09.621 --> 00:08:11.189
-I got a lot of friends.
I have a lot of friends.

00:08:11.189 --> 00:08:12.691
-You just go,
"Yeah, I have a lot of friends.

00:08:12.691 --> 00:08:14.626
As soon as you go to bed,
I'm out on the town."

00:08:14.626 --> 00:08:16.295
-That's right.
-Thanks so much for being here.

00:08:16.295 --> 00:08:18.931
Congrats on an incredible run
on the show.

00:08:18.931 --> 00:08:20.065
Thank you so much.
-Thank you, thank you.

00:08:20.065 --> 00:08:21.733
-You guys, that's Laura Linney.

00:08:21.733 --> 00:08:23.870
The final season of "Ozark" is
streaming now on Netflix.

